Q: Static Extension Method: CS0704: Cannot do non-virtual member lookup in &#39;type&#39; because it is a type parameter? A: <p>To avoid confusion, let's rewrite your original code like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static class IBinaryIntegerUtilityExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;(T)
        where T : IBinaryInteger&lt;T&gt;
    {
        /// &lt;summary&gt;
        /// Return the number of bytes in a integer type
        /// Note that this is the same method name and intent for the instance version supplied by the framework
        /// &lt;/summary&gt;
        public static int MyGetByteCount() =&gt; T.Zero.GetByteCount();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>So that we know <code>MyGetByteCount()</code> is actually the one we defined, not the <code>GetByteCount()</code> from interface <code>IBinaryInteger</code>.</p>
<p>Using the <code>extension</code> keyword to introduce a bunch of extension methods <strong>without</strong> the parameter is only for the type, not instances. I notice that because this one will pass:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>int numBytes = short.GetByteCount();
</code></pre>
<p>But this one will fail:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>short shortValue = 0;
// CS0176 Member 'IBinaryIntegerUtilityExtensions.extension&lt;short&gt;(short).MyGetByteCount()' cannot be accessed
int numBytes = shortValue.GetByteCount();
</code></pre>
<p>To define an extension method for instances of type <code>T</code>, we need to use the old way of defining extension methods:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static class IBinaryIntegerUtilityExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;(T)
        where T : IBinaryInteger&lt;T&gt;
    {
        /// &lt;summary&gt;
        /// Return the number of bytes in a integer type
        /// Note that this is the same method name and intent for the instance version supplied by the framework
        /// &lt;/summary&gt;
        public static int MyGetByteCount() =&gt; T.Zero.GetByteCount();
    }

    /// &lt;summary&gt;
    /// Return the number of bytes of an integer type.
    /// &lt;/summary&gt;
    /// &lt;typeparam name=&quot;T&quot;&gt;The type in question, should implement IBinaryInteger.&lt;/typeparam&gt;
    /// &lt;param name=&quot;value&quot;&gt;An instance of such type.&lt;/param&gt;
    /// &lt;returns&gt;The count of bytes of such type.&lt;/returns&gt;
    public static int MyGetByteCount&lt;T&gt;(this T value)
        where T : IBinaryInteger&lt;T&gt;
    =&gt;
        value.GetByteCount();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or, merge the old style extension method into the extension block, but the extension block header needs a minor change:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static class IBinaryIntegerUtilityExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;(T value)  // need to specify &quot;value&quot; here
        where T : IBinaryInteger&lt;T&gt;
    {
        /// &lt;summary&gt;
        /// Return the number of bytes in a integer type
        /// Note that this is the same method name and intent for the instance version supplied by the framework
        /// &lt;/summary&gt;
        public static int MyGetByteCount() =&gt; T.Zero.GetByteCount();

        /// &lt;summary&gt;
        /// Return the number of bytes of an integer instance.
        /// &lt;/summary&gt;
        public int MyGetByteCount2() =&gt; value.GetByteCount();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that we cannot use the same name <code>MyGetByteCount</code>, it would generate a CS0111 error (already defines a member).</p>
<p>The newly added one works on instances of the type <code>T</code>.</p>
<p>Your <code>MyClass&lt;T&gt;</code> generic class needs to use this new one, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class MyClass&lt;T&gt;
    where T : IBinaryInteger&lt;T&gt;
{
    // public int NumBytes =&gt; T.GetByteCount();
    public int NumBytes =&gt; default(T)!.MyGetByteCount2();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that the <code>!</code> null-forgiving operator is there because without it the IntelliSense would nag about <code>default(T)</code> could be null.
But since we are only working on number types which are all value types (structs), you might as well change the generic class definition like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class MyClass&lt;T&gt;
    where T : struct, IBinaryInteger&lt;T&gt;
{
    // public int NumBytes =&gt; T.GetByteCount();
    public int NumBytes =&gt; default(T).MyGetByteCount2();
}
</code></pre>
<p>After the changes, you can compile the following without error:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var c = new MyClass&lt;short&gt;();
int numBytes = c.NumBytes;
</code></pre>

Q: Insert record with foreign key if primary key is missing or not? A: <p>Something like this:</p>
<pre><code>CREATE PROCEDURE insert_primary_and_secondary(primary_val VARCHAR(100), secondary_val INT)
BEGIN
    DECLARE primary_id INT;

    BEGIN TRANSACTION; -- ensure atomic

    -- step 1
    SET primary_id = (SELECT id FROM primary_table WHERE col1 = primary_val);
    IF primary_id IS NULL -- step 2
    THEN
        INSERT INTO primary_table (col1) VALUES (primary_val);
        SET primary_id = LAST_INSERT_ID();
    END IF

    -- step 3
    INSERT INTO secondary_table (fk_col, col2) VALUES (primary_id, secondary_val);

    COMMIT;
END
</code></pre>
<p>You can of course add additional parameters to the procedure for all the column values that need to be inserted. This is just the minimal version.</p>

Q: Game - Calculate player&#39;s diagonal movement A: <p>Since you don't use a <a href="https://github.com/rokobuljan/gamepad" rel="nofollow noreferrer">joystick controller</a> (which returns a wider range of angle values) but only <strong>keyboard arrow keys</strong> to move your character <strong>diagonally</strong>, you don't need anything special but</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// If single arrow key:
distance = 1

// If combo arrow keys:
distance = 1 / Math.sqrt(2)     // ~0.707
</code></pre>
<p>which will give you the needed diagonal movement distance of <code>~0.707</code><br />
and once you've defined your distance value, multiply it by the speed:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>distance *= speed;
</code></pre>
<p>the rest is then pretty straightforward:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>if (K[37]) Player.x -= distance;
if (K[38]) Player.y -= distance;
if (K[39]) Player.x += distance;
if (K[40]) Player.y += distance;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/iMjeb.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/iMjeb.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p><strong>Demo example</strong>:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const Player = {
  el: document.getElementById('player'),
  x: 200,
  y: 100,
  speed: 2,
  move() {
    // You will use canvas, this is for demo only
    this.el.style.transform = `translate(${this.x}px, ${this.y}px)`;
  }
};

const K = {
  fn(ev) {
    const k = ev.which;
    if (k &gt;= 37 &amp;&amp; k &lt;= 40) {  // If is arrow
      ev.preventDefault();
      K[k] = ev.type === "keydown";
    }
  }
};

const update = () =&gt; {
  let dist = K[38] &amp;&amp; (K[37] || K[39]) || K[40] &amp;&amp; (K[37] || K[39]) ? 0.707 : 1;
  dist *= Player.speed;
  if (K[37]) Player.x -= dist;
  if (K[38]) Player.y -= dist;
  if (K[39]) Player.x += dist;
  if (K[40]) Player.y += dist;
  Player.move();
}

document.addEventListener('keydown', K.fn);
document.addEventListener('keyup', K.fn);

(function engine() {
  update();
  requestAnimationFrame(engine);
}());</code></pre>
